WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.960
Human rights are not static. They evolve
alongside scientific progress.

2
00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:09.360
This timeline tracks how our legal and
ethical priorities have shifted over

3
00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:14.320
time. Early generations established
fundamental liberties and social equality,

4
00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:18.420
rights designed to protect the physical
person within a traditional society.

5
00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:23.080
Today, AI clinical research operates
within the fourth generation of rights.

6
00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:27.500
This is the domain of technoscience,
covering developments in biology,

7
00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:29.320
genetics, and information technology.

8
00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:34.260
Fourth generation rights center on
identity, establishing protections for our

9
00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:38.140
genetic and digital data. On a subjective
level, this ensures that

10
00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:42.140
technologies like neurotech do not degrade
an individual's internal sense of

11
00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:46.720
self. On an objective level, it establishes
that biological and digital data

12
00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:50.740
are fundamental parts of a person's
identity, requiring the same level of

13
00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:52.600
protection as their physical body.

14
00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:56.700
As these technologies become more social,
we move toward a fifth generation of

15
00:00:56.740 --> 00:01:00.400
rights, focusing on democracy and good
governments in a highly automated

16
00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:04.320
world. Historically, we protected bodies
from physical harm.

17
00:01:04.460 --> 00:01:10.092
Today, we must protect the digital and
genetic identity that AI now processes.
